{"format":"margin-bench/play-episode/1","env":{"id":"dsp.scale_readout","title":"Weighing scale readout","domain":"dsp","seed":0},"agent":{"kind":"scripted","name":"lowpass","settings":null},"prompt":"You are commissioning the readout of a digital weighing scale. The hardware is simulated; treat it as a real bench.\n\nHardware\n- A load cell under a 0..50 kg platform, an amplifier and an ADC sampling at 6400 Hz. The samples arrive already scaled to kilograms.\n- The installation is electrically noisy. Nothing else about the interference is known: measure it.\n- The display updates 10 times per second and shows the newest output sample of your processing chain.\n\nProcessing chain. You build it from these blocks, applied in order, in float64 arithmetic. Each block's cost is in multiply-adds per sample at its own input rate; the total, referred to the ADC rate (a block after decimation by 8 costs an eighth), must stay within 12 per ADC sample.\n- moving_average(n): mean of the last n samples. Cost 2 (running sum).\n- notch(f0_hz, q): second-order IIR notch. Cost 5.\n- lowpass(kind, order, fc_hz): IIR lowpass; kind butter, bessel or cheby1 (0.5 dB ripple); order 1..8; fc_hz is the -3 dB point for butter and bessel, the ripple edge for cheby1. Cost 5 per second-order section.\n- fir_lowpass(taps, fc_hz, window): windowed-sinc FIR with unity gain at DC; window rectangular, hann, hamming or blackman. Cost = taps.\n- decimate(factor): keeps every factor-th sample and drops the rest; it does no filtering of its own. Cost 0. Blocks after it run at the lower rate. The chain output rate must stay at least 10 Hz.\nA new chain starts from rest (all states zero). With no chain the display shows raw ADC samples. At most 10 blocks.\n\nInstruments: a spectrum analyzer and an oscilloscope, on the ADC signal or on the chain output, and calibration weights for the platform. Right now the platform is empty and there is no chain.\n\nAcceptance test. It runs after you call submit, with the chain you leave:\n1. The chain starts from rest, the platform empty. At t = 2 s a weight goes on (5..45 kg). 4 s later it is swapped for another (5..45 kg). 4 s after that the platform is emptied, and the test ends 4 s later.\n2. Requirements, on the display readings:\n- after each change, within +/-0.05 kg of the true load no later than 0.5 s, and staying there until the next change;\n- over the last 2 s before each change and before the end: standard deviation of the readings at most 0.0033 kg, and the mean within +/-0.01 kg of the true load.\n\nYou have 20 minutes of signal time (each capture and run uses its own duration) and at most 40 tool calls. Work like an engineer: measure, design, check on the bench with known weights, then call submit.\n","tools":["set_chain","spectrum","scope","set_load","run","submit"],"status":"done","error":null,"stop_reason":"submitted","started_at":"2026-10-16T06:09:45+00:00","finished_at":"2026-10-16T06:09:45+00:00","wall_s":0.03,"model_s":0.0,"usage":{"model_calls":0,"prompt_tokens":0,"completion_tokens":0,"reasoning_tokens":0,"cost_usd":0.0},"steps":[{"i":0,"wall_s":0.02,"kind":"model","sim_t":0.0,"text":"The display is jumpy. A second-order Butterworth lowpass at 5 Hz should smooth it and still respond fast.","reasoning":null,"calls":[],"latency_s":null,"tokens":{"prompt":null,"completion":null,"reasoning":null},"cost_usd":null,"finish_reason":null},{"i":1,"wall_s":0.02,"kind":"tool","name":"set_chain","args":{"blocks":[{"type":"lowpass","kind":"butter","order":2,"fc_hz":5.0}]},"result":{"t_s":0.0,"blocks":[{"type":"lowpass","kind":"butter","order":2,"fc_hz":5.0,"rate_in_hz":6400.0}],"cost_per_adc_sample":5.0,"budget":12.0,"output_rate_hz":6400.0},"error":null,"summary":"butter2(5 Hz); cost 5/12 per ADC sample, output 6400 Hz","alert":false,"sim_t0":0.0,"sim_t1":0.0},{"i":2,"wall_s":0.02,"kind":"tool","name":"set_load","args":{"kg":20},"result":{"t_s":0.0,"load_kg":20.0},"error":null,"summary":"platform load 20 kg","alert":fa